{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790715827,"nonce":"0fLh0m1Jp4etwwW5I2zO8Xx4ay0=","title":"Investigate interpreter and JIT trajectory divergence in physics workloads","message":"ChimpMonk's three-bone ragdoll validation produces different trajectories across Ash execution modes after floor impacts. The same compiled PhysicsChecks bytecode passes its physical bounds in each mode, but a pose checkpoint at frame 60 of the heavy-end case is about 27.3770 in JIT and SIMD AOT versus 27.4361 in interpreter and hybrid. Later contact paths diverge further; final pose checksums for that case are approximately 36.18 in JIT, 36.79 in hybrid, and 37.08 in interpreter. Small floating-point differences may explain chaotic contact divergence, but the consistent early checkpoint gap warrants isolating where the interpreter and JIT first disagree.\n\nReproduce from the sibling ChimpMonk checkout at the ragdoll validation commit:\n\n    haxe -cp . -cp example/src -main PhysicsChecks -hl /tmp/chimpmonk-physics.hl\n    ash --mode jit /tmp/chimpmonk-physics.hl\n    ash --mode interp /tmp/chimpmonk-physics.hl\n    ash /tmp/chimpmonk-physics.hl\n\nThe scene and guide use the default hybrid mode and tolerate the observed trajectory spread while checking finite state, joint bounds, contacts, and sleep/wake. Investigate the first divergent operation with a smaller reproducer. Compare f32 rounding, transcendental math, and native calls across interpreter and JIT before deciding whether a runtime change is appropriate. This issue does not claim bitwise-deterministic physics across modes.","files":null}]}